        {"type": "prose", "heading": "Airports and stations: what to know", "html": "<p>Charles de Gaulle is about forty kilometres north-east of Paris: allow forty-five minutes to well over an hour by car depending on traffic, or about thirty-five minutes on the RER B suburban train to the centre. Orly, to the south, is closer, but the journey depends a lot on the final destination and the time of day. Beauvais, used by some low-cost airlines, is more than an hour away. Paris taxis apply regulated flat fares from the airports, which avoids nasty surprises, but the queue can be long at peak times and nobody is waiting for your employee at the exit.</p><p>For arrivals by train, Gare du Nord, Gare de l'Est, Gare de Lyon and Montparnasse are all in central Paris and well connected to the metro. A chauffeured transfer remains worthwhile for people with luggage, late arrivals and housing outside Paris. We choose the most sensible option for each arrival with you, and write it into the quote.</p>"},
